# ==============================================================================
# qai-backend — Environment variable reference
# ==============================================================================
#
# This file is a CURATED template, not a complete dump of every env var the
# service reads. The authoritative list lives in the Zod schema at
# `src/config/environment/env.ts` — open that file to find every variable,
# its validation rule, and its default.
#
# What belongs here: env vars that DevOps needs to set or tune in production
# and that contain NO secrets. As new tunables ship, append a clearly-marked
# section below — don't silently rewrite existing sections, that creates
# merge conflicts during PR review.
#
# What does NOT belong here: secrets (DB password, API keys, Telegram tokens,
# etc.). Those live in the host's secret manager or `.env.local` (gitignored).
#
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Section: Phase 4 (May 2026) — Mobile regression concurrency tunables
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Copy the lines you want to override into your prod / staging / dev env.
# Values are read inside `concurrency-decision.ts` via a NaN-safe parser
# (Number.isFinite + >=1); garbage values fall back to the documented
# default rather than crashing the boot.
#
# IMPORTANT: do NOT raise REGRESSION_MOBILE_MAX_CONCURRENCY above 1 until
# Phase 3 (host-ADB mutex, branch `fix/host-adb-mutex` in qait-mobile-runner)
# is shipped to prod AND stress-tested for 7 days. Without it, parallel
# regressions race the daemon socket and surface `Client aborted` /
# `pm create-user failed`. See regression-run.service.ts:2079-2087 for the
# full pre-Phase-3 incident notes.
#
# Decision policy lives in
# `src/services/testing/regression/concurrency-decision.ts` —
# every env var here is an INPUT to that function, not a hard switch.
# Final cap is always min(MAX_CONCURRENCY, runnerPoolIdle, desiredCap).

# --- Hard ceiling (operator force-cap) ----------------------------------------
# Min 1. Sets the absolute maximum for ANY regression run regardless of pool
# state or test count. Default 1 preserves pre-Phase-4 serial behaviour, so
# this entry is a no-op until you explicitly bump it.
#
# Rollout suggestion:
#   - Week 1 (post Phase 3 deploy): keep at 1, verify Phase 4 log line
#     `[REGRESSION_RUN] Mobile concurrency decided` appears with cap=1
#   - Week 2: bump to 2, watch cold_start_count metric stay flat
#   - Week 3+: bump to 3 if pool size >= 3 and no race incidents
REGRESSION_MOBILE_MAX_CONCURRENCY=1

# --- Test-count split between small and large batches -------------------------
# testCount <= threshold → use SMALL_CAP. testCount > threshold → use LARGE_CAP.
# Default 5 means: 1-5 tests counted as small, 6+ as large. Tune up if you
# typically run larger regressions and want them to get the bigger cap.
REGRESSION_MOBILE_SMALL_BATCH_THRESHOLD=5

# --- Desired cap for small batches (testCount <= SMALL_BATCH_THRESHOLD) -------
# Subordinate to MAX_CONCURRENCY and runner poolIdle.
REGRESSION_MOBILE_SMALL_CAP=2

# --- Desired cap for large batches (testCount > SMALL_BATCH_THRESHOLD) --------
# Subordinate to MAX_CONCURRENCY and runner poolIdle. Usually equal to your
# runner's POOL_ANDROID_SIZE so a large regression saturates the pool.
REGRESSION_MOBILE_LARGE_CAP=3
SNAPSHOT_USE_CDP_PREVIEW=true
SNAPSHOT_USE_CDP_BACKGROUND=true

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Section: Scheduler hosting
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The scheduler bundle (per-minute schedule sweep + auto-learn retire +
# account-deletion purge + stale-run reaper + retention sweeps) must run in
# EXACTLY ONE place.
#
#   - Local docker-compose: a dedicated `scheduler` container runs it. Leave
#     this flag FALSE so the worker doesn't also run it.
#   - Prod: the dedicated scheduler container is NOT deployed (only api + worker
#     are). Set this TRUE on the WORKER service so the bundle runs there,
#     guarded by a Redis leader lock (src/scheduler/leader.ts) — exactly one
#     worker replica runs the bundle even at N>1. Do NOT set it on the API.
#
# If both the scheduler container and the worker run it against one Redis, the
# leader lock still guarantees a single live bundle — but that's a backstop,
# not the intended topology. Pick one host.
SCHEDULER_IN_WORKER=false

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Section: Self-serve PLG signup (May 2026)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Master switch for the B2C self-serve flow. Default false keeps the existing
# B2B behaviour (business-email gate + manual admin approval) fully intact.
#
# When true:
#   - new users are auto-approved on first auth (status='approved') and get a
#     default workspace + project, so they skip the onboarding wizard
#   - personal-email signups (Gmail etc.) are accepted; disposable still blocked
# The requireApproved middleware stays in place — flipping this off restores
# the gated flow with no code change. Implemented in
# `src/services/core/self-serve-provisioning.service.ts` +
# `src/services/auth/auth.service.ts`.
# Default false here so copying .env.example verbatim does NOT silently enable
# self-serve. Set to true per-deploy to turn on the B2C flow.
AUTO_APPROVE_SIGNUP=true

# ─── Companion (physical-device relay, ADR-0009) ────────────
# Lets a user run editor/regression tests on their OWN physical Android phone
# (attached to their laptop) instead of a shared emulator. The phone's local
# runner dials OUT to this backend over the /companion WebSocket relay.
#
# To test the companion feature locally, set all three below AND
# MOBILE_RUNNER_ENABLED=true (above). RUNNER_USER_TOKEN_SECRET must be the SAME
# value the companion's local runner uses. Full flow:
# qait-mobile-runner/COMPANION_TESTING.md
COMPANION_ENABLED=true
# Any strong random string — a SEPARATE secret from RUNNER_USER_TOKEN_SECRET
# (separate audience). Companion access tokens are HMAC-signed with this.
COMPANION_TOKEN_SECRET=dev-companion-secret-change-me
# Must match the runner's RUNNER_USER_TOKEN_SECRET so the proxied X-User-Token
# verifies on the companion's local runner.
RUNNER_USER_TOKEN_SECRET=dev-runner-user-token-secret-change-me

# ─── Mobile app binary storage (ADR-0008) ───────────────────
# APK/IPA binaries live in AZURE ONLY. Supabase is legacy: it still serves
# reads for objects uploaded before the cutover, but must never receive a new
# binary. Set this to `azure` — including locally.
#
# The `supabase` value is retained only for the legacy path and is a trap on a
# dev machine: reads under it are Supabase-ONLY (the dual-read fallback runs
# azure→supabase, never the reverse), so every build uploaded since the
# cutover is invisible and any run that must install one dies with
# `Blob not found at mobile-apps/… (supabase)` before a single step executes.
# That failure hides easily — MobileAppPrepService skips the download entirely
# when the package is already on the emulator, so the editor keeps working and
# only a cold-device regression run breaks. See
# .claude/lessons/20260728-prod-uploaded-apk-is-invisible-locally.md
#
# Both are left UNSET below so copying this file verbatim keeps legacy reads
# working. Writing a binary is gated separately (`getBlobStoreForWrite`) and
# fails with a message naming this variable, so an unset provider can no
# longer put an APK in the wrong store — it just can't upload one.
#
# You need the two vars + Azure auth as soon as you run the stack LOCALLY
# against Azure-stored builds, because your machine then IS the api/worker.
# In prod those run on Container Apps under Managed Identities that hold the
# RBAC grants; a laptop has none, so `DefaultAzureCredential` has to fall
# through to the Azure CLI. No account key is involved:
#   1. brew install azure-cli && az login
#   2. your Azure principal needs `Storage Blob Data Reader` on the account
#      (`Storage Blob Data Contributor` to upload, plus `Storage Blob
#      Delegator` to mint signed URLs). qait-infra grants these to the
#      api/worker/gitlab managed identities ONLY — a human principal needs its
#      own assignment; ask whoever owns the subscription.
#   3. uncomment the two vars (account name: qait-infra/azure/terraform/.env)
# Leave AZURE_CLIENT_ID unset locally — it only disambiguates the
# user-assigned identity on Container Apps.
# MOBILE_APP_STORAGE_PROVIDER=azure
# AZURE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT=
# AZURE_STORAGE_CONTAINER=mobile-apps
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=   # Container Apps only — do not set locally

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Section: Monitoring — Sentry (errors + tracing + logs)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Wiring lives in src/instrument.ts (imported FIRST in src/index.ts). An empty
# SENTRY_DSN keeps the SDK disabled — the expected state for local dev / tests.
# The DSN is environment-specific config; treat it like a secret — put the real
# value in .env.local (gitignored) or the host secret manager, not here.
#
#   Sentry project: qaitdev · Express SDK (onboarding wizard).
#
# SENTRY_DSN=
# Traces sample rate 0..1. 1.0 captures every transaction (fine for dev); lower
# it in production (e.g. 0.1) to control performance-event volume.
SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=1.0
